<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">What Is Lumbar Spondylosis?</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Lumbar spondylosis is a degenerative condition of the lower spine marked by progressive wear of the discs, the small joints at the back of the spine and the vertebrae themselves. It develops gradually as the discs lose water content and height, which reduces the cushioning between the vertebrae. Over time the body may respond by forming small bony spurs along the edges of the vertebrae and those small joints may thicken.</p>
  <p>The process can affect several structures at once. Loss of disc height alters how weight is distributed across the spine. Changes in the small spinal joints produce stiffness and localised pain. In more advanced presentations the spinal canal or the openings where the nerves exit may narrow, which can affect the nerve roots leaving the lower spine. <a href="/pages/ayurvedic-lumbar-spondylosis-treatment-in-hyderabad" style="color:#3c8dbc">Lumbar spondylosis ayurveda</a> understands these changes as part of a wider pattern of tissue depletion rather than as isolated mechanical damage.</p>
  <p>It is worth being clear about what this condition is not. Lumbar spondylosis is not the same as lumbar spondylolisthesis, where one vertebra slips forward over another and it is not the same as <a href="/pages/ayurvedic-cervical-spondylosis-treatment-in-hyderabad" style="color:#3c8dbc">cervical spondylosis</a>, which is the equivalent degenerative process in the neck. The three differ in mechanism, presentation and management and KSAC Hospitals assesses and treats each separately.</p>
</section>

<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Types of Lumbar Spondylosis</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <h3 style="text-align:center;font-size:20px;font-weight:600;color:#2c3e50;margin-bottom:16px">By Severity</h3>
  <div class="cs-types-grid">
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Mild Degenerative Changes</h4>
      <p>Early loss of disc water content with minimal height loss and small bony spurs. Patients may have intermittent lower back stiffness, particularly after sitting for long periods or on waking. The small spinal joints may show early wear without significant functional limitation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Moderate Spondylosis with Disc Narrowing</h4>
      <p>Noticeable loss of disc height at one or more levels, with moderate spur formation and thickening of the small spinal joints. Patients typically report persistent lower back pain, longer morning stiffness and reduced flexibility in bending forward and turning. This is the presentation most commonly seen at KSAC Hospitals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Severe Spondylosis with Canal Narrowing</h4>
      <p>Advanced disc degeneration, prominent spurs and significant joint change that may narrow the spinal canal or the openings where nerves exit. Patients may have pain radiating into the legs, numbness, tingling or weakness in the lower limbs alongside their back symptoms. Presentations at this end of the range are assessed with particular care, since nerve involvement changes what treatment is appropriate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 style="text-align:center;font-size:20px;font-weight:600;color:#2c3e50;margin:32px 0 16px">By Spinal Level</h3>
  <div class="ls-level-grid">
    <div class="ls-level-card">
      <h4>L3-L4 Involvement</h4>
      <p>Degenerative change at the third and fourth lumbar vertebrae. Pain and stiffness are usually felt in the mid to lower back.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ls-level-card">
      <h4>L4-L5 Involvement</h4>
      <p>One of the most commonly affected levels, because of the load and movement demands at this segment. Degeneration here may contribute to lower back pain and, where a nerve is compressed, symptoms radiating into the thigh and leg.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ls-level-card">
      <h4>L5-S1 Involvement</h4>
      <p>The junction between the lower spine and the pelvis carries considerable weight load and is frequently affected. Change at this level may produce deep lower back pain and, where nerves are involved, symptoms extending into the foot.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

