<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Common Causes That Contribute to Lumbar Spondylosis</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Lumbar spondylosis develops through a combination of age-related wear and contributing lifestyle, occupational and constitutional factors. Commonly recognised causes include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Age-related wear: the primary driver is the natural degenerative process in the discs, spinal joints and vertebrae over decades, with disc hydration decreasing progressively from the thirties onward</li>
    <li>Sedentary routine: lack of regular activity weakens the muscles alongside the spine and reduces the support they give it, placing disproportionate stress on the discs and joints</li>
    <li>Prolonged sitting: long periods seated, particularly in postures that flatten the natural inward curve of the lower back, place sustained compressive load on the lumbar discs</li>
    <li>Occupational strain: work involving repetitive bending, heavy lifting, exposure to vibration or sustained awkward posture increases the rate of degenerative change</li>
    <li>Excess body weight: additional weight increases the load on the lower spine and shifts the centre of gravity forward, increasing demand on the lumbar structures</li>
    <li>Prior injury: earlier <a href="/pages/ayurvedic-disc-bulge-treatment-in-hyderabad" style="color:#3c8dbc">disc herniation</a>, a vertebral fracture or significant muscle injury may accelerate degenerative change at the affected level</li>
    <li>Genetic predisposition: individual variation in disc structure and spinal alignment influences the rate and severity of degeneration</li>
  </ul>
</section>
